# Line endings.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true system-wide, which stores LF in the
# repository but checks CRLF out into the working tree. Every time a tool writes
# an LF file (most editors, and anything scripted) Git warns on `git add`:
#
#   warning: in the working copy of 'X', LF will be replaced by CRLF ...
#
# Pinning eol=lf keeps the working tree and the repository both at LF, so no
# conversion happens in either direction and the warnings stop. The repository
# content is unchanged by this: it was already LF.
#
# text=auto still lets Git detect and skip binary files.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the exception -- cmd.exe requires CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary types, listed pre-emptively: none are tracked today, but sample
# response data and brush textures are plausible additions.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
